    {
      "rank": 1,
      "ballot_id": "B018",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexei Kitaev",
        "Andrew M. Steane",
        "Peter W. Shor"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Quantum error correction showed how quantum information can be protected despite decoherence, turning quantum computing from a fragile principle into a physically testable programme. The underlying ideas have had broad influence across quantum physics.",
        "attribution": "Shor and Steane independently established early quantum error-correcting codes; Kitaev developed the topological-code approach that connected protection to many-body physics. The three contributions are distinct and complementary.",
        "maturity": "The theory is well established and experiments have demonstrated error correction and early fault-tolerant operations. Large-scale fault-tolerant computing remains a goal, but the physics has already been tested substantially.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery combines several theoretical advances, and the precise boundary between foundational coding ideas and later developments could be drawn differently."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B057",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Alexandre Blais",
        "Andreas Wallraff",
        "Robert J. Schoelkopf"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Circuit QED established a powerful regime for coupling superconducting artificial atoms to microwave photons, enabling precise control and measurement of light-matter interactions and underpinning a major platform for quantum information.",
        "attribution": "Blais developed the central theoretical framework, while Wallraff and Schoelkopf led landmark experiments that established and extended the architecture. The credited group captures key theory and experimental contributions, though the broader community was substantial.",
        "maturity": "The platform has been reproduced across many laboratories and has supported increasingly complex quantum experiments. Its scientific importance is established even though scalable error-corrected machines are not yet complete.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The achievement overlaps historically and scientifically with other foundational work on superconducting circuits, including the 2025 Nobel-recognized subject."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B016",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Juan Ignacio Cirac",
        "Peter Zoller",
        "Rainer Blatt"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Trapped-ion proposals and experiments established a route to controllable quantum logic, while ultracold-atom work broadened the reach of quantum simulation. The work helped make quantum information a precision experimental science.",
        "attribution": "Cirac and Zoller made foundational theoretical proposals for quantum logic and simulation; Blatt demonstrated and developed trapped-ion operations experimentally. The ballot title also includes ultracold atoms, which broadens the achievement beyond this trio's strongest shared contribution.",
        "maturity": "Ion-trap quantum logic has been demonstrated and refined for decades, with independent groups confirming key results. Practical scaling and error correction remain active challenges.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The combined framing spans distinct platforms, so the credited contributions do not map equally well to every part of the discovery."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B023",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Carlton M. Caves",
        "H. Jeff Kimble",
        "Nergis Mavalvala"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Squeezed light provides a direct way to reduce measurement noise below the standard quantum limit and has improved precision measurements, including gravitational-wave detection. It links a deep quantum-optics principle to consequential experiments.",
        "attribution": "Caves developed foundational theory of quantum limits and squeezing; Kimble advanced optical generation and control; Mavalvala helped establish its use in gravitational-wave detectors. The field's progress also depended on substantial collaborative experimental work.",
        "maturity": "Squeezing is established in laboratories and operating observatories, with sustained improvements in detector sensitivity. Its practical and scientific value is well confirmed.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The contribution is distributed across theoretical proposals, optical techniques, and large detector collaborations, complicating individual attribution."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B006",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Charles L. Kane",
        "Eugene J. Mele",
        "Laurens W. Molenkamp"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum spin Hall effect and topological-insulator framework revealed robust electronic states protected by topology, opening a major new direction in condensed matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele made the key theoretical predictions; Molenkamp's experiments on HgTe quantum wells provided an early decisive observation. These roles form a strong and understandable theory-experiment attribution.",
        "maturity": "The original effect and broader topological-insulator phenomena have been studied in many materials and devices. Some proposed applications remain difficult, but the discovery's scientific significance does not depend on them.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The boundaries of the recognized discovery could include a wider set of theoretical and experimental contributors."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B065",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Hidetoshi Katori",
        "Jun Ye"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks combined ultrastable optical transitions with trapping that suppresses motion-related shifts, transforming precision timekeeping and enabling sensitive tests of fundamental physics.",
        "attribution": "Katori developed the lattice-clock concept and demonstrated its central advantages; Ye made major independent advances in clock performance and precision spectroscopy. The two contributions are complementary and clearly linked to the achievement.",
        "maturity": "Optical clocks have undergone repeated independent improvements and are now among the most precise measurement devices available. Their metrological impact is established, while some applications are still developing.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The field has advanced through many laboratories, and a prize focused on the original invention may underrepresent later essential contributions."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Ataç İmamoğlu",
        "Pascale Senellart",
        "Peter Michler"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "On-demand semiconductor quantum-dot sources made single photons available in a controlled and scalable solid-state setting, an important achievement for quantum optics and photonic quantum technologies.",
        "attribution": "İmamoğlu's proposals and work on cavity-enhanced quantum-dot emission helped define the field; Senellart and Michler led influential experimental demonstrations and development. Their contributions represent theory and experiment, though many groups advanced source performance.",
        "maturity": "Reliable single-photon emission has been demonstrated and improved across platforms. Indistinguishability, collection efficiency, and integration continue to limit some uses, but the basic physical achievement is robust.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "It is difficult to select a small number of individuals from a fast-moving international effort with several distinct device milestones."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B005",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Georges",
        "Dieter Vollhardt",
        "Gabriel Kotliar"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory supplied a broadly useful framework for strongly correlated electrons and reshaped the theoretical study of materials where ordinary band theory fails.",
        "attribution": "Vollhardt and Georges developed central formulations and applications, while Kotliar made major contributions to the framework and its connection to material-specific calculations. The credited theorists represent leading contributions to a collaborative development.",
        "maturity": "The method has been used and extended for decades, with extensive application to real materials and continuing theoretical development. Its influence is well established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The framework's development involved a broad community, and its status as a singular discovery is less sharply defined than a discrete experimental result."
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
